/T (Scam messages are longer and carry more URLs \(on average 123 vs 102 characters,and 67 % vs 21 % contain a link\); url_risk_max has the strongest correlation with the label \(0.58\), followed by has_url \(0.44\), lex_threat \(0.40\), lex_urgency \(0.35\) and n_chars \(0.34\). )
/E (Scam messages are longer and carry more URLs \(on average 123 vs 102 characters,and 67 % vs 21 % contain a link\); url_risk_max has the strongest correlation with the label \(0.58\), followed by has_url \(0.44\), lex_threat \(0.40\), lex_urgency \(0.35\) and n_chars \(0.34\). )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
784 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 104 0 R
/T (Scams hit the urgency, threat, prize, verify and action lexicons far more often, but legitimate messages also contain money and sensitive words \(bank alerts, bill reminders\), so a single lexicon count is not enough on its own. upper_ratio has a long right tail, yet an all-capitals message can be legitimate \(a friend writing in capitals\), so capitals alone are not a scam signal. The heatmapshows that the strongest links are between closely related columns \(for example has_url and url_risk_max,0.79\); the other correlations are moderate. )
/E (Scams hit the urgency, threat, prize, verify and action lexicons far more often, but legitimate messages also contain money and sensitive words \(bank alerts, bill reminders\), so a single lexicon count is not enough on its own. upper_ratio has a long right tail, yet an all-capitals message can be legitimate \(a friend writing in capitals\), so capitals alone are not a scam signal. The heatmapshows that the strongest links are between closely related columns \(for example has_url and url_risk_max,0.79\); the other correlations are moderate. )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
